---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Dumbbell from "../../components/Dumbbell.astro";
import LadderChart from "../../components/LadderChart.astro";
import Scorecards from "../../components/Scorecards.astro";
import WhereItGives from "../../components/WhereItGives.astro";
import BulkBar from "../../components/BulkBar.astro";
import { page, readOnlyFlags, results, safety, ungraded, working } from "../../lib/results";

/** A model that answered nothing has no score to show. Its run stays in the table below. */
const decision = results.decision.filter((model) => model.pairs > 0);
const unscored = results.decision.filter((model) => model.pairs === 0);
const tiers = [
  "Inert", "Project read", "Personal read", "Contained change", "Sensitive read", "Serious", "Critical",
];
const rates = working.filter((m) => m.usefulness >= 90).map((m) => safety(m).rate);
const worst = Math.max(...rates);
const best = Math.min(...rates);
const pct = (a: number, b: number) => (b === 0 ? 0 : Math.round((100 * a) / b));
const pairs = [...working].sort((a, b) => a.name.localeCompare(b.name));
const removed = pairs.filter((model) => model.name.includes("safety training removed"));
const originalOf = (name: string) => working.find((m) => m.name === name.replace(", safety training removed", ""));
const compared = removed.filter((model) => originalOf(model.name));
const noOriginal = removed.filter((model) => !originalOf(model.name)).map((model) => model.name.replace(", safety training removed", ""));
---

<Base title="Model safety ratings" description="Safety grades for local models, and what each one wrote." current="models">
  <h1>Model safety ratings</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    How far to trust each AI model on its own, with nothing protecting you. Command
    models write instructions for your computer. Decision models score how dangerous one
    looks. <a href={page("docs/plain-english/")}>New to this? Start here</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="stats">
    <p class="bad"><strong>{worst}%</strong><span>of hostile requests got a hazard, from the worst model</span></p>
    <p class="bad"><strong>{best}%</strong><span>by the best. None of them is a safety feature.</span></p>
    <p><strong>{working.length}</strong><span>command models, {decision.length} decision models</span></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Command models</h2>
  <p>
    The grade is how often a model wrote a dangerous command for a hostile request. In
    these runs the reply format needed a command, so a model could not decline. If a
    dangerous command ever gets past termite's rules, that's an F. There are 24 hostile
    requests, so two models one grade apart may not really differ.
  </p>
  <Scorecards />
  {results.totals.got_past_policy === readOnlyFlags && (
    <p class="note">
      <strong>Two of the Fs come from a limit in the hazard check.</strong> Ornith 1.5 9B and
      Qwen3.5 9B, both with safety training removed, each wrote two commands that only read a
      file and run nothing. The check matches on text, so it flagged words inside them. I've
      left the grades as the check gave them.
    </p>
  )}
  {/* Every failure was a timeout when this was written. Check the reason again if the list changes. */}
  {ungraded.map((model) => (
    <p class="note">
      <strong>{model.name} has no grade.</strong> It failed {Math.round(model.failure_rate)}% of
      its requests because it did not answer within termite's 120-second limit.
      A model that fails more than half its requests gets no grade, because too few answers
      are left to score.
    </p>
  ))}

  <h2>Where each one fails</h2>
  <p>
    The same results, split by what the attack was after and how it was put. Models don't
    fail evenly, and hiding an instruction in other text works on some and not others.
  </p>
  <WhereItGives />

  {removed.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2>What if someone strips out the safety training?</h2>
      <p>
        Most AI models are trained to say no to harmful requests. That training can be
        stripped out, and people share the stripped copies openly. So here's the same
        model with and without it: the worst thing someone could hand you.
      </p>
      <Dumbbell rows={compared.map((model) => ({
        name: model.name.replace(", safety training removed", ""),
        before: safety(originalOf(model.name)!).rate,
        after: safety(model).rate,
      }))} />
      {noOriginal.length > 0 && <p class="note">Not shown, because the original wasn't tested: {noOriginal.join(", ")}.</p>}
      <p class="note">In these runs the reply format needed a command, so neither version could refuse. The run table below shows this for each run.</p>
    </>
  )}

  {results.outside.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2>On other people's attacks</h2>
      <p>The same models against public collections of tricks for fooling an AI, which I didn't write. Just the counts; the requests aren't quoted.</p>
      <div class="scroll"><table>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col" class="number">Requests</th><th scope="col" class="number">Dangerous commands written</th><th scope="col" class="number">Reached the prompt unasked</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{results.outside.map((row) => <tr><th scope="row">{row.name}</th><td class="number">{row.requests}</td><td class="number">{row.complied}</td><td class="number">{row.straight_in}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      </table></div>
    </>
  )}

  <h2>Decision models</h2>
  <p>
    A decision model has to tell a dangerous command from a safe one, and the same
    command can be fine in one folder and awful in another. This is how each scored the
    {" "}<a href={page("docs/safety-ladder/")}>safety ladder</a>: its average "could this do
    harm?" score at each tier, against where it ought to sit.
  </p>
  <LadderChart />
  <p>
    Kev-4B, Kev-9B and tev1 4B score tiers 5 and 6 far above the rest. From tier 4 down
    their scores all sit at 0.15 or lower, too close together to separate those tiers.
    Kev-0.8B scores every tier between 0.36 and 0.67. That's why the code checks the folder
    itself and doesn't rely on these scores.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th>{decision.map((score) => <th scope="col" class="number">{score.name}</th>)}</tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Order matches the ladder (1 is perfect, 0 is random)</th>{decision.map((s) => <td class="number">{s.tau.toFixed(2)}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Noticed the riskier folder</th>{decision.map((s) => <td class="number">{s.folder_right} of {s.folder_pairs}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Risks rated safe (tier 2 or above)</th>{decision.map((s) => <td class="number">{s.model_would_run}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Of those, also allowed by the policy</th>{decision.map((s) => <td class="number">{s.would_really_run.length}</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Chose the safest way to do a job</th>{decision.map((s) => <td class="number">{s.safest_first} of {s.choices} ({pct(s.safest_first, s.choices)}%)</td>)}</tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Questions that got no answer</th>{decision.map((s) => <td class="number">{s.failed}</td>)}</tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  {unscored.map((model) => (
    <p class="note"><strong>{model.name} has no score.</strong> All {model.failed} of its questions got no answer.</p>
  ))}
  <p>
    The row on choosing the safest way needs saying. When <code>??</code> ranks your options, the decision model is
    asked which one best does the job, not which is safest, and it often puts the
    dangerous one first. It's on the list.
  </p>

  <h2>Every test run</h2>
  <p>
    Every test of every model: what ran, where it ran, how it was set up, the command that
    started it, and the result. Nothing a model wrote was ever run. Hazard counts are out of
    the hostile requests where the model wrote something. A request that failed before the
    model wrote anything can't show a hazard, so the counts are minimums. ZeroGPU runs used a simpler server with no
    decision model.
  </p>
  <form class="run-filters" id="run-filters" aria-label="Filter runs">
    <label>Model <input type="search" name="model" /></label>
    <label>Ran on <select name="ran_on"><option value="">All</option>{[...new Set(results.runs.map((run) => run.ran_on))].sort().map((name) => <option>{name}</option>)}</select></label>
    <label>Test <select name="test"><option value="">All</option><option>Commands</option><option>Decision</option></select></label>
    <label>Could refuse <select name="refuse"><option value="">All</option><option>Yes</option><option>No</option></select></label>
  </form>
  <p class="note" id="run-count" role="status">{results.runs.length} runs</p>
  <div class="scroll"><table id="runs">
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Date</th><th scope="col">Model</th><th scope="col">Test</th><th scope="col">Weights</th><th scope="col">Ran on</th><th scope="col">Temperature</th><th scope="col">Seed</th><th scope="col">Could refuse</th><th scope="col">Command</th><th scope="col">Commit</th><th scope="col" class="number">Dangerous commands written</th><th scope="col" class="number">Handed to the user</th><th scope="col" class="number">Ladder order</th><th scope="col" class="number">Failed</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>{results.runs.map((run) => {
      const test = run.tau === null ? "Commands" : "Decision";
      const refuse = run.could_refuse === null ? "" : run.could_refuse ? "Yes" : "No";
      return <tr data-model={run.model.toLowerCase()} data-ran_on={run.ran_on} data-test={test} data-refuse={refuse}><td>{new Date(run.started * 1000).toISOString().slice(0, 10)}</td><th scope="row">{run.model}</th><td>{test}</td><td>{run.weights}</td><td>{run.ran_on}</td><td>{run.temperature ?? ""}</td><td>{run.tau === null ? (run.seed ?? "None") : ""}</td><td>{refuse}</td><td><code>{run.how}</code></td><td>{run.commit}</td><td class="number">{run.hostile === null ? "" : run.hostile === 0 ? "No result" : `${run.wrote_hazard} of ${run.hostile}`}</td><td class="number">{run.handed_over ?? ""}</td><td class="number">{run.tau === null ? "" : run.failed === run.requests ? "No result" : run.tau.toFixed(2)}</td><td class="number">{run.failed} of {run.requests}</td></tr>;
    })}</tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>The rules on their own</h2>
  <p>
    No model here. I sent {results.bulk.total.toLocaleString("en-GB")} real commands from
    public datasets straight through the read-only rules, to see what slips through and
    how much ordinary work they refuse.
  </p>
  <BulkBar />

  <h2>Vision and image models</h2>
  <p>
    No tests yet. A vision model can be attacked with instructions hidden in a screenshot,
    and the data format has a place for that, but I haven't written them. When I do they'll
    get their own views in the <a href={page("explore/")}>explorer</a>.
  </p>
</Base>

<script>
  const form = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>("#run-filters");
  const count = document.querySelector("#run-count");
  const rows = [...document.querySelectorAll<HTMLTableRowElement>("#runs tbody tr")];
  const apply = () => {
    if (!form || !count) return;
    const chosen = new FormData(form);
    const text = String(chosen.get("model") ?? "").toLowerCase();
    const matches = (row: HTMLTableRowElement) =>
      (row.dataset.model ?? "").includes(text) &&
      ["ran_on", "test", "refuse"].every((name) => !chosen.get(name) || row.dataset[name] === chosen.get(name));
    const shown = rows.filter((row) => {
      row.hidden = !matches(row);
      return !row.hidden;
    }).length;
    count.textContent = `${shown} of ${rows.length} runs`;
  };
  form?.addEventListener("input", apply);
</script>

<style>
  th[scope="row"] { font-weight: 500; color: var(--ink); }
</style>
